WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.330
Today, this woman’s known as Rosie the Riveter.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:08.940
Her poster says “we can do it.”

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:14.030
During World War II, overall American women’s
employment increased from 12 million in December

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:18.830
1941 to 16 million in March 1944.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:21.730
It peaked at 19 million that July.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:28.000
More than 2,000,000 women started jobs in
wartime manufacturing specifically — the

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.530
stuff of riveting.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.910
They came from other industries, housework,
and school.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:37.290
How did millions of women enter a new industry
in the span of a few years?

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.030
“Here is the office of the supervisor of
Women Employees.”

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.870
“Women in steel are simply the result of
realistic thinking.”

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:49.810
“Women of steel” changed labor and helped
win a war.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:54.899
But how did the country manage to transform
a massive war effort on the turn of a dime?

00:00:54.899 --> 00:00:59.140
How did all the women behind this image become
riveters?

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:12.390
“With the army taking men by the thousands,
more than 16,000 from our plant so far, we

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.830
had to find people to replace them.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:19.970
A great untapped reserve was women.”

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:26.380
The Pearl Harbor attack effectively launched
the United States into World War II.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.730
The labor pool had to get bigger.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:29.730
Quickly.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:33.200
A group of women in government wanted women
to fill in the gaps.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.420
In the Department of Labor, Frances Perkins
was the first female secretary of labor.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.150
She worked with Thelma McKelvey of Women’s
Labor Supply Services, she was part of the

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:47.259
war production board, which managed the conversion
of peacetime industries to meet wartime needs.

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:51.570
Mary Anderson was the leader of the Women’s
Bureau, a Department of Labor agency that

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.920
advocated for women’s employment since 1920.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:02:00.570
They all worked with the War Manpower Commission,
a wartime agency that had a women’s advisory

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.829
committee, including leaders across industries
like efficiency expert Lillian Gilbreth, lawyer

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:09.690
Margaret Hickey, and school principal Maudelle
Bousfield.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.860
These groups all helped shape public perception
of wartime women workers with ads and PR,

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:20.260
but the most important process was practical:
they had to help women find the thousands

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.150
of war industry jobs that needed workers.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:29.500
They did that through the US Employment Service.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.290
This pamphlet lays out the steps women could
take to find a wartime job.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:38.860
The Office of War Information Distributed
it to magazine editors and the public.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.810
Government work: go to the Civil Service.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:45.090
Military: work in shore stations through a
reserve like WAVES.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.500
Industry: find the US Employment Service.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.950
This agency became a subset of the War Manpower
Commission.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:57.379
The US Employment Service coordinated local
offices, which referred job seekers to employers

00:02:57.379 --> 00:02:59.880
who could offer war industry work.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.599
Employers also recruited through classified
ads.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:06.530
Trade schools advertised to men and women
as well.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:11.599
As the draft further depleted the supply of
male workers, women filled a host of industrial

00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:17.019
jobs, from lathe work to welding.

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:23.540
One specific job was perfect for that boom:
As early as May, 1942, Thelma McKelvey said

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.920
that for aircraft jobs, women riveters in
particular would be commonplace.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:35.300
“With the ever-increasing demand for greater
speeds in aircraft, it has been necessary

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:42.080
to remove every possible projection from the
outer surface of the airplane.”

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.190
Industrial jobs clustered around Detroit,
Baltimore, and Seattle, with high aircraft

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.720
and ship production.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.950
Increases in women in manufacturing in those
cities were huge — especially for:

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:56.970
“Riveters.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:03.400
Learning how and where to put the 700,000
rivets that go into a single Liberator bomber.”

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.840
To train an onslaught of inexperienced labor,
employers developed techniques.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.280
Some of them were likely sexist:
“They were as fast as men if not faster,

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:17.349
for rivets are the buttons of a bomber to
hold it together against a speed of nearly

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:19.479
350 miles an hour.”

00:04:19.479 --> 00:04:25.520
Others sped up training for employees who
had to learn an entirely new job really quickly.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.150
“Women workers can be surprisingly good
producers.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:35.330
You’ve got to study every job and subdivide
it into simple operating steps.’

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.830
Mary Anderson of the Women’s Bureau recommended
riveting, “which is the most common job

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.229
throughout assembly.”

00:04:41.229 --> 00:04:46.669
The Women’s Bureau recruited for these jobs
in ways that appealed to contemporary notions

00:04:46.669 --> 00:04:53.059
of women’s traits: riveting used “a delicate
touch, manipulative dexterity of a high degree,

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:56.080
as well as extreme accuracy in measurement.”

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.770
Teamwork could help with training.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:03.749
Rosalind Palmer, who inspired the name Rosie
the Riveter, started out as a riveter but

00:05:03.749 --> 00:05:08.250
became a welder after she was paired with
a “crackerjack welder” who “taught me

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:09.250
all he knew.”

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.120
The training showed results.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.099
Boeing Seattle quadrupled monthly output from
1942-1944.

00:05:15.099 --> 00:05:21.569
In Detroit, worker hours per bomber dropped
at the Ford Willow Run Bomber Plant.

00:05:21.569 --> 00:05:27.599
They went from 200,000 to less than 18,000
hours, thanks in part to increased efficiency

00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:29.939
from subdividing jobs.

00:05:29.939 --> 00:05:35.150
These riveters succeeded in transforming the
war effort — and the labor market.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.759
Rosie the Riveter is truth and myth.

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:38.759
Look at this pin.

00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:43.669
“We Can Do It” was a poster the Westinghouse
Electric company made to briefly show at its

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:44.740
factories.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:49.439
The real-life woman who inspired her likeness
was an Alameda Naval Air station employee

00:05:49.439 --> 00:05:51.960
named Naomi Parker.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:58.759
Rosie became mythologized in a Norman Rockwell
painting, a song — “Rosie, the Riveter”

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:00.819
— and even a movie shortly thereafter.

00:06:00.819 --> 00:06:06.280
And the Westinghouse poster became all-but-ubiquitous
when a copy was unearthed in the 1980s.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:12.099
But the truth about real riveters was more
complicated, and it didn’t make the poster.

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:18.622
In 1942, Thelma McKelvey of the War Production
Board testified that women were paid 10 to

00:06:18.622 --> 00:06:24.179
15 cents an hour lower, despite equal pay
regulations.

00:06:24.179 --> 00:06:29.110
Results varied wildly by company and region,
but during the war, some unions used claims

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:33.749
of seniority and job differentiation to keep
women’s pay down.

00:06:33.749 --> 00:06:38.120
They also pushed for women to give back “men’s
jobs” when the war was over.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.159
“How you like your job Mrs. Stoner?”

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:41.610
“How about after the war?

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.659
Are you going to keep on working?”

00:06:43.659 --> 00:06:48.319
“I should say not, when my husband comes
back, I’m going to be busy at home.”

00:06:48.319 --> 00:06:49.319
“Good for you.”

00:06:49.319 --> 00:06:51.310
“What about after this war, Lee?”

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:57.270
“Well, this job belongs to some soldier,
and when he comes back, he can have it.”

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:58.639
“Ah that’s swell.”

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:02.990
Women of color were also discriminated against
in some places.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:08.809
Black women in industry went from 6.5% to
18% during the war.

00:07:08.809 --> 00:07:10.300
But employers were spotty.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:18.319
At Wagner Electric in St. Louis, 64% of employees
were white women, 24% black males, and 12%

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:19.319
white males.

00:07:19.319 --> 00:07:22.520
They simply did not hire black women.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:27.529
Most women who did work were already in the
workforce, single, and without children, so

00:07:27.529 --> 00:07:31.639
it’s easy to overestimate how much the war
changed things.

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:35.749
Total working women also declined when the
war ended.

00:07:35.749 --> 00:07:40.250
But World War II did transform public and
private sector labor.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:46.249
About half the already-employed women switched
employment from clerical work to higher paying

00:07:46.249 --> 00:07:47.599
manufacturing work.

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:52.770
And the number of employed married women increased
during and after the war.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:58.419
The real story of women riveters is more complicated
than a poster or a slogan.

00:07:58.419 --> 00:08:05.119
But together, private industry, women leaders,
and all those working women changed work in

00:08:05.119 --> 00:08:12.769
America — one rivet at a time.

